4
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:55,200
Well, that's a research that you can do quickly, go through all of his literature, arrive at something.

5
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:57,520
We had another girl here from Syria.

6
00:00:58,320 --> 00:01:10,160
She was studying manuscripts and she discovered that one manuscript that had been attributed to a certain author was not, could not have been his, it had to be someone else.

7
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:14,640
And she could tell by the quality of the language and then even date things within the text.

8
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:26,320
Well, again, it was the digitized process that allowed her to see that quickly, rather than having to read through 400 books and go to Paris and go to different countries and compare the volumes.

9
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:37,280
Father David, Matthew, it's a real pleasure to be with you this morning.

10
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:46,080
We're here in Rome for as we get ready to launch the Alexandria Digitization Hub, which is a huge project that you guys have been working on for a very long time.

11
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:53,840
Father David, if I may begin with you, can you share the genesis of that project and your collaboration with Matthew?

12
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:55,280
Sure.

13
00:01:55,440 --> 00:01:58,000
It's a series of births in a certain sense.

14
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:03,840
It didn't start out as one idea and then we were moving the train out of the station to arrive at another location.

15
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:10,320
For us, the library of the Orientali, the Pontifical Oriental Institute, is unique in the world.

16
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:14,480
There's no library anywhere that covers all of the Eastern churches.

17
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:20,800
So the Eastern churches which come out of Constantinople, the first church in Jerusalem is an Eastern church and so on.

18
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:24,800
So our library covers all of the churches.

19
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:29,920
And when you say covers all of the churches, it means it covers languages, it covers history., it covers politics.

20
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:36,320
Very often the first thing that was written down was the Bible in another language or translated.

21
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:43,200
When missionaries went from one country to another, they had to translate the Bible, they had to structure even the alphabet in different languages.

22
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:48,880
So the Bibles become great sources of language, language development and structure and all.

23
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:57,120
So we have quite a number of academics, researchers that come to the Orientale to use the library, the resources of the library.

24
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:58,560
We have students.

25
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:00,080
It's basically a graduate school.

26
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:10,400
But the concern was not many can come to Rome for a week or a month and use the resources, isn't there a way to make these resources more available?

27
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:17,200
And we're talking about a geographical area from Russia to Ethiopia, from southern Italy to southern India.

28
00:03:17,840 --> 00:03:19,280
That's where the Eastern churches are.

29
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,440
But even Western churches, bishops, scholars are interested in these resources.

30
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:32,560
So my first interest was to digitize the books, get the books online for our students, our past students, for researchers, for scholars.

31
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:37,840
We could even have a combined thing online of several scholars studying a manuscript.

32
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:39,200
That would be an excellent thing.

33
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:41,840
We have perhaps five hundred manuscripts in the library.

34
00:03:42,560 --> 00:03:43,760
So that's how it began.

35
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:48,640
And we found some friends and benefactors, much of it through Matthew's help and inspiration.

36
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:58,720
People came here, sent big scanners, people came from New York, experts in digitizing, and began the process of digitizing our own publications.

37
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:12,000
And it was kind of magical, like in a certain sense it was simple what they were doing, high density photographs of everything they were digitizing, 600 DPI we could reduce to PDFs and make them available.

38
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:22,080
But immediately there was the question, it would be nice if these could be researched by word and not just pulling down a book or pulling down an article.

39
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:29,360
So as we went on digitizing, we bought a scanner, we got another scanner, and we had two people hired to scan more and more books.

40
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,640
And then we were looking initially at one set of software that was being developed that would allow researchers to do research on a manuscript.

41
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,000
But it was really with the arrival of AI that transformed our possibilities.

42
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,360
So first getting the text off of a PDF.

43
00:04:48,080 --> 00:04:52,080
onto a computer language that could be easily researched by word without errors.

44
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:59,600
But then our library has, I don't know if it has 20 or 30 languages in it, ancient languages, modern languages.

45
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:03,840
And so if you want to research something, you want to cross the languages.

46
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,680
So we might have the works of John Chrysostom in four different languages.

47
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,160
And even that has a value for scholars.

48
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:13,120
How do they translate this word into another language?

49
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:31,040
Again, when you look at history, the arguments over the interpretations of the nature of Jesus, of the creeds, a word like person in English, in Italian, sounds pretty straightforward, but the word persona in Greek means it can mean the mask that you wear in a play.

50
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:33,360
So is that the best translation to understand?

51
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:41,120
So you see the debates of scholars from 1000, 1500 years ago trying to deal with these changes.

52
00:05:41,520 --> 00:06:02,080
Well, again, to be able to put these things online, you use AI technology so that you're vectorized, you're looking for concepts rather than words in one language, so you take the word nature or person and conceptually you translate that into all the languages that are available to the AI program and you search through the entire library.

53
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:07,200
So the richness for research and for analysis is really quite extraordinary.

54
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:09,520
Wow, that's that's so inspiring.

55
00:06:09,680 --> 00:06:26,400
And then Matthew, when Father David came in with these, with these, you know, this goal of digitizing the, the patrimony of the church and making the content and the books available from the Orientali to the students around the world, what were some of the technical challenges that you faced?

56
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:32,240
Well, I mean, I think when we started the project, we weren't thinking so big as like the patrimony of the church.

57
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,880
I mean, that's something that we now are focused more on.

58
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:40,400
But yeah, initially it was just, let's just try to make sure that these resources in the Eastern church are made more available.

59
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:43,600
And then, you know, there was always the idea that if it worked, then we could expand it.

60
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:46,240
There were a bunch of technical challenges.

61
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:47,200
I mean, you know, one is.

62
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:48,560
One is the books themselves.

63
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:52,640
And Father David can speak more to this than I can.

64
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:56,160
But many of these books were very different from each other, right?

65
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:58,080
Some had very delicate bindings.

66
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:00,800
Some of the more modern books, not so much.

67
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:03,040
Some of them are very large books, some of them are very small.

68
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:07,200
And so, as much as possible, you want the robot to do most of the work here, right?

69
00:07:07,360 --> 00:07:10,800
With the arm, which the robot arm which turns the pages and does the scanning for you.

70
00:07:10,880 --> 00:07:17,840
So we're trying to automate the process as much as possible, kind of reduce the human in the loop and just to allow us to increase the rate at which we're scanning.

71
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:24,160
But obviously, every time you change the book out, you have to kind of redial in the robot.

72
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:27,120
so it understands what kind of text is working with so it can effectively turn the pages.

73
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,120
So I know that was that was a challenge.

74
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:37,200
And I know that the two lovely women who work in there have become something of experts at kind of sizing up books and learning how to dial in the robots.

75
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:41,840
So there's still very much like a collaboration between, you know, the robots and the human beings at this stage.

76
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:50,160
But, you know, the hope is that we'll be able to figure out new ways and deliver kind of new technologies into the hub which will allow for further and further automation.

77
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:54,960
So we'll be less dependent on people, you know, in this process and that will allow us to scan more.

78
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:58,720
So I would say that would be one technical challenge that continues to this day.

79
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:01,440
I will just say that we're starting to look at some interesting approaches.

80
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:13,440
I'm sure some people may have seen some articles about how laser technologies and now kind of like CT scanning are being used to kind of see through books and especially like manuscripts which are very delicate, right, which they're afraid to kind of open up for the purposes of scanning.

81
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:22,480
And so there's new technologies on the scene, I think that show a lot of promise, which would allow us to scan the books without actually having to necessarily open them up and turn the pages, which in some cases could damage them.

82
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:24,960
So I think the future is very bright for digitization.

83
00:08:26,080 --> 00:08:46,160
I would say that the second kind of biggest challenge was, and fellow David kind of spoke to it, is how do we extract the information from the pages and then make sure that because, you know, OCR, optical character recognition., which is something anyone using Adobe PDF is familiar with because it allows it's what allows you to take an image and then allow it to select a text, right?

84
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:51,360
That's because Adobe programs, you know, run this optical character recognition on it, but it's a bit flaky, right?

85
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:57,200
So if you were to go out and copy all the text from a PDF document typically and paste it into a text, it's a bit of a mess, right?

86
00:08:57,360 --> 00:08:58,880
There can be all kinds of weird characters in there.

87
00:08:59,040 --> 00:08:59,920
There's no formatting.

88
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:03,840
And I'm talking about like, these are the kind of simpler problems.

89
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:06,480
For more complex texts, there can be all kinds of artifacts and things.

90
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:13,440
And so, one of the human in the loop challenges was that someone had to go, as you're trying to extract information from the PDFs and clean it up.

91
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:15,040
You had to kind of clean up all the noise.

92
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:17,440
Then in many cases, you had to kind of label the text.

93
00:09:17,600 --> 00:09:19,840
You know, what's a header, like what's a footnote?

94
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:21,280
You had to kind of put that information in.

95
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:25,440
And you had to do all this labeling before you kind of would go through this process of then digitizing it.

96
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:30,560
And I say digitizing kind of the classical sense, not in the AI sense.

97
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:52,080
And so what we were trying to figure out is, is there a way that we could run this extraction and basically bring AI into the game so it could actually handle all the cleanup, all the labeling of, you know, the text and then also handle automatically this process of converting it from first to kind of vector embeddings, which kind of makes this text language agnostic for people to query.

98
00:09:52,480 --> 00:09:57,360
And then finally to a format called TEI, which is like an archiving standard, which is kind of a rich metadata format.

99
00:09:57,760 --> 00:10:15,680
This is all to say that this whole process of figuring this out was something that we felt if we could crack this and have AI do this part of the job, this would allow us to significantly speed up digitization, mainly because there's a lot of scanned content around the world scanned for archiving purposes, but there's never been extraction run on it, which means you couldn't really effectively query it.

100
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:24,960
So if we figured if we could if we focused here on this on this particular problem, this could allow us to unlock all those PDFs which are just kind of sitting around for storage purposes.

101
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:27,920
And we and to date we've been able to pretty much automate that process.

102
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:29,280
That's extremely good.

103
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:38,880
And Father David, of course, the library is at the center of the university and the educational programs that their students from around the world are coming.

104
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:49,440
How has this project impacted their studies, like making these texts available for students who for whatever reason might not be able to attend class or come to Rome physically?

