00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:47,120
That's extraordinary.

144
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:59,120
And then Matthew, as we get ready to start to really maximize the hub as the scanners come in and as they start to really the work comes underway, what is that going to look like?

145
00:14:59,360 --> 00:15:02,000
Can you break it down and kind of walk us through those steps?

146
00:15:03,120 --> 00:15:03,280
Sure.

147
00:15:04,400 --> 00:15:09,600
So we embarked on this kind of proof of concept, I think it's been almost a couple of years now.

148
00:15:09,920 --> 00:15:14,080
So we had this kind of robot scanner and we had this kind of high-end manual scanner for more delicate manuscripts.

149
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:17,760
And we really just want to kind of push the technology, see how far we could get with it.

150
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:24,000
And so I mean, we still think there's a long way for the hardware to go, but certainly the robot scanner is helpful.

151
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:26,880
So we said, okay, so it's working.

152
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:29,600
We need to figure out this whole digitization process.

153
00:15:29,920 --> 00:15:36,640
And that's kind of the last year, what we've been really working on is how far can we push AI in this whole, this whole automated ingestion process.

154
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:40,000
And I think we've gotten close to cracking it.

155
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:41,360
There's still some work to do, obviously.

156
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:49,760
You know, the father David mentioned to it, I mean, one of the unique challenges in this ingestion work is, you know, some of these books have languages which are ancient, right?

157
00:15:50,160 --> 00:16:00,000
And so what this means effectively for AI is the AIs are not able to embed them, which is basically kind of convert them to map the text semantically effectively, right?

158
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:01,920
And basically convert it into memory.

159
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:10,800
And that's mainly because embedding models are trained on large corpuses of data, like large language models.

160
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:20,720
And if their training data, the distribution of their training data, doesn't consist of large swaths of that of, let's say, a particular language, it's just not capable of effectively embedding it.

161
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:28,800
So that doesn't mean that the AI is useless at it, that they may still get it fifty percent or sixty percent correct because they can look at the relationships between other languages and they can kind of make inferences.

162
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:31,040
But there's still a long way to go there.

163
00:16:31,200 --> 00:16:40,560
So I think for most of the conventional languages that are spoken today and some of the ancient languages like Latin, Greek, the AI project is still ongo working very effectively.

164
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:47,280
So, and I think that, of itself, is going to be enormously beneficial, especially considering, because the church's historical language has been Latin, so there's a lot written in it.

165
00:16:47,680 --> 00:17:01,280
But I think there is an opportunity here to kind of tap into these experts in these ancient languages and help them to kind of develop training data sets so we can kind of trade into the basic, trade a better embedding model, which is more capable of understanding these languages and therefore mapping them more effectively.

166
00:17:02,080 --> 00:17:06,800
So, that's a research project that I'm looking forward to embarking with the Pontifical Universities in Rome.

167
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:10,960
But so, the immediate next step is, let's get more scanners.

168
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:12,160
Let's just speed this up, right?

169
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,800
There's a lot of books here among some Pontifical Universities.

170
00:17:15,200 --> 00:17:18,800
