00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:16,560
So this is quite extraordinary to think that a book that was just literally scanned by a robot within a few hours is then available to anywhere in the world to scan and drive insights from.

181
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:29,520
So, and I think once people realize, you know, ultimately what this will mean for scholarship and I think just generally, you know, generally, just for even for us ordinary folks, right, who are just looking for some insight from the church to help us in our daily lives.

182
00:18:30,480 --> 00:18:31,760
I think it will garner a lot of attention.

183
00:18:31,920 --> 00:18:44,560
And then what we're hoping is we can then raise potentially some funds philanthropically, maybe from the grassroots, from some donors and foundations to expand the scanner fleet and hopefully invest in development of even better hardware.

184
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:54,240
The final phase that I'm looking forward to, and I say final in quotation marks because I don't really know where this project will ultimately go, but obviously having the hub in Rome is exciting.

185
00:18:54,640 --> 00:18:58,000
There's a lot of libraries, a lot of content here, and we'll be scanning for years.

186
00:18:58,240 --> 00:19:05,680
But there's obviously this, there's great repositories of information, materials that should be scanned, documents, books, all over the place.

187
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:08,960
So the question is, how do we get the scanners to them?

188
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:17,520
And so one of the things we're looking at now is kind of development of a flying hub, where we build some scanners which are a little bit easier to take apart and reassemble.

189
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:22,720
And in that way the scanners can go to the works and that will allow us to get our hands on more and more.

190
00:19:23,120 --> 00:19:23,840
It's extraordinary.

191
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:31,680
And then, Father David, I mean, we know that there's there's there's there's so many resources, there's so many important books and documentation here in Rome.

192
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:38,960
Working, you know, within the Pontifical University system, this is something that, you know, from my eyes, seems like it's very much at the forefront.

193
00:19:39,120 --> 00:19:39,760
It's an innovation.

194
00:19:39,920 --> 00:19:42,400
It's something that I don't know of any other university doing this.

195
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:57,040
What is it, what has it been like for you bringing this mission, bringing this, you know, this really unique and new idea to the Pontifical Universities and to the Eternal City, which doesn't have the reputation of always being the quickest adapter?

196
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:19,440
Well, it is true, when we were conceiving some aspect of this project, the first books that we were digitizing and how can we access them, I looked around the world, Europe and North America at big universities that had resources, and no one had a program that could do anything better than OCR reading of a PDF file, which shocked me.

197
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:21,600
I mean, we're not heavily funded.

198
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:27,680
We don't have $15 billion in reserve as a lot of universities, bigger universities would have.

199
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:34,800
So, but at the same time, I was concerned that the church should be on the cutting edge of this stuff.

200
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:39,680
So, again, it's just, it's providencial, it's serendipitous.

201
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:41,120
Matthew and I knew each other.

202
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:46,640
Here we had other people who were interested, people offering their scanners for this trial experiment sort of thing.

203
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:54,720
So even though the AI technology is changing rapidly, digitizing technology is changing.

204
00:20:55,040 --> 00:20:57,360
It could be more rapid, but it's changing.

205
00:20:57,360 --> 00:21:08,560
It's important for the church to be on the front of this kind of thing, even to give direction about where it should or shouldn't go is one thing to be making its resources available.

206
00:21:08,720 --> 00:21:13,760
I mean, the church is about the gospel, which is the word and communication and creating community.

207
00:21:14,160 --> 00:21:16,880
So the more broadly that we can do that, the better it is.

208
00:21:17,760 --> 00:21:24,560
I wouldn't say it's a risk, but it's a kind of nervous excitement that the technology is changing so quickly.

209
00:21:25,280 --> 00:21:28,480
We would not have imagined where we are today, say, two years ago.

210
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:29,840
That's for sure.

211
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:33,440
And the rapid changes in AI, the first AI program was magical.

212
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:33,680
Wow.

213
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:38,800
The second iteration was, Wow, now it can do this.

214
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:47,360
And then the third iteration, so we're chasing something, but we're chasing something that makes sense, that already has a fruit, you know.

215
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:49,760
So, and who knows where we'll go.

216
00:21:49,920 --> 00:22:03,280
Matthew is mentioning that languages we study here some, let's call them weird languages, dead languages, Numidian, Scobodian, that you could all of the people that can read these languages, you could put into one small classroom, you know.

217
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:06,960
And yet important documents are written in these languages.

218
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:10,480
Now, they have languages today that share the same root.

219
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:30,480
But how do you understand that language in its context only through other literature that was written, which is again the importance of having not just scripture in that language, but all the other books of politics or whatever geography science from that language to understand the connotations that are in the word at the time.

220
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:46,800
And the latest programs Google is very big in this, but not only Google is analyzing a language that nobody really knows with the benefit of languages that have already been studied ancient languages and giving indications how to break down this language and how to understand it.

221
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:48,240
So again, even that sort of thing.

222
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:51,520
is very informative for the kind of research that we're doing here.

223
00:22:51,920 --> 00:23:10,240
So being at the forefront, kind of being at the cutting edge, introducing these new technologies in a way that is having absolute good, absolutely good results, and not worrying about, are they going to clone babies, are they going to scan dogs, you know, or something like that, but showing that good example of how the technology can be used.

224
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:11,520
That's extraordinary.

225
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:19,520
And then, Father, speaking on that potential for good, we know that, of course, if you just turn on the news today, there's obviously lots of conflict, lots of tension.

226
00:23:20,240 --> 00:23:25,440
And of course, in the Oriental sphere, there's, there's, there's, there's.

227
00:23:25,680 --> 00:23:27,360
There is tension and violence and stuff.

228
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:51,920
With the AI being able to help with documentation, communication, clearing up and making evident aspects of translations, traditions and just making that scholarship available on a global stage, it seems like the potential for ecumenical dialogue, interreligious dialogue, true scholarship across boundaries, it seems like the potential is immense for that.

229
00:23:52,240 --> 00:24:00,400
Can you speak a little bit on that importance and how this technology, the scanners, the hub, Magisterium AI, is going to be able to contribute to that?

230
00:24:01,120 --> 00:24:01,200
Yeah.

231
00:24:02,560 --> 00:24:08,320
It's strong and it's weak at the same time because a lot of it depends on human beings.

232
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:11,760
So if human beings don't wanna, they won't.

233
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:16,080
If human beings are presented with the truth they don't want to hear, they won't listen.

234
00:24:16,560 --> 00:24:17,360
So that's there.

235
00:24:18,240 --> 00:24:21,280
But on the other hand, the other aspect is absolutely true.

236
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:32,560
Once it's there in reliable documentation, once it's there in demonstrable fact, once it's there historically and anybody can look at it and research it, it is harder to get away with a lie.

237
00:24:33,440 --> 00:24:36,640
You still can, but it is harder to get away with a lie.

238
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:40,560
And people of goodwill on both sides of an argument will find that.

239
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:47,760
And the example I gave of the churches having come together, this happens once the ground is clear.

240
00:24:48,880 --> 00:24:51,920
But the problem is always human beings.

241
00:24:54,080 --> 00:25:03,520
But again, the opening up the truth, opening up the facts, they become harder and harder to resist when they're broadly available and not just locked in a library.

242
00:25:03,840 --> 00:25:09,840
And father, can you share a little bit on how your students are interacting with these digitized documents?

243
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:15,440
How, you know, maybe in the classroom or if they're going abroad for a visit or for an exchange program.

244
00:25:17,920 --> 00:25:24,320
How is this digital technology coming into the classroom in a practical sense and helping them, you know, in their everyday studies?

245
00:25:24,560 --> 00:25:26,880
Yeah, I would say we're still early in that.

246
00:25:27,280 --> 00:25:34,000
And obviously the younger generation is much more adept at the technology than the professors who are in the classroom.

247
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:39,680
So the, I would say right from the beginning, three professors were gung ho about it.

248
00:25:40,240 --> 00:25:42,320
The other fifty, well, you know.

249
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:53,920
And then ironically, COVID came and then there were major conversions among the other professors when they saw what the technology could do, both the visual technology in the classroom and then the access to books.

250
00:25:54,480 --> 00:26:18,480
So I would say as it's still in a kind of experimental stage, not in the sense that it's an experiment that we'll reject, but we're learning as we go and for the professors to learn how to integrate digitized resources into study processes, into textual research and all of that, and affirming or having cautions or challenging students in this or that direction.

251
00:26:19,680 --> 00:26:23,680
And again, more and more students wanting to come here to have access to the resources.

252
00:26:24,240 --> 00:26:28,560
But we're still at a learning stage of how better to make these things available.

253
00:26:28,880 --> 00:26:29,680
For example, one thing that.

254
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:36,880
we couldn't do so easily before, we would have to have a conference and bring in a bunch of professors from around the world to study a manuscript.

255
00:26:37,200 --> 00:26:38,480
And now they can do that online.

256
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:41,040
They can make a copy of a certain text.

257
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:48,960
All of them can annotate it in their various languages and have this kind of visual discussion over a text online, go back and forth with it.

258
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:52,400
The document is saved with all the annotations, come back to it in a week.

259
00:26:52,960 --> 00:27:02,240
So the possibility that's already there, but the possibilities for enhanced high-end academic research on documents is really, really quite, quite rich.

260
00:27:03,200 --> 00:27:06,880
And then, Father David, can you share a little bit about how you've transformed?

261
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:13,920
the Pontifical Oriental Institute, brought new technologies, made recording digital access to the classrooms.

262
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:15,360
If you could share that for audience.

263
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:18,160
There are funny things with technology.

264
00:27:18,480 --> 00:27:27,040
When I was in Canada and I worked with native people on a reserve in northern Ontario, and all of a sudden mobile phones came out.

265
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:28,720
And I grew up in Toronto.

266
00:27:28,880 --> 00:27:36,080
And here the irony was that everyone in Toronto had a mobile phone, had a phone, a stationary phone at home, and a network of phones in their office.

267
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:39,440
And we were up in the bush and have no way of communicating with one another.

268
00:27:39,600 --> 00:27:42,720
You have to drive two hours to give a message to someone.

269
00:27:43,040 --> 00:27:43,840
And that's where..

270
00:27:44,320 --> 00:27:49,920
the technology was needed, not in the city, but so it's now everywhere.

271
00:27:50,160 --> 00:28:03,760
Well, there was something similar here is that in at universities, I would say generally, but maybe more specifically in Rome, come to us and you can enjoy our resources, which is easier said than done for like 99% of the world.

272
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:09,440
So again, the whole thing of when I came here, there was not much technology in the building.

273
00:28:09,840 --> 00:28:14,560
Just in principle, I wanted to have technology, modern technology, in all of the classrooms.

274
00:28:15,200 --> 00:28:18,080
And again, I could find some resources to get this done.

275
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:20,720
Again, I would say most of the professors.

276
00:28:21,520 --> 00:28:26,560
here said, well, if he can do it, okay, we're not going to stand in the way, let him go ahead and do this.

277
00:28:26,880 --> 00:28:30,320
And again, two or three loved it, saw the advantage of using it.

278
00:28:30,480 --> 00:28:32,560
And we were using Google and Google for Education.

279
00:28:33,840 --> 00:28:41,840
And the professors who could understand the technology quickly, they enhanced learning for the students right away in the classroom.

280
00:28:42,400 --> 00:28:46,880
So we put in television screens.

281
00:28:47,040 --> 00:28:52,000
We don't have any overhead projectors anymore, but it's all large screens in the classrooms.

282
00:28:52,480 --> 00:28:55,840
We brought in Google technology, microphones and speakers.

283
00:28:56,160 --> 00:29:00,320
And it was a big classroom, we might have six sets throughout the classroom.

284
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:05,040
If it was a smaller classroom, there might be two sets of speakers and microphones.

285
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:08,400
And again, it was high quality stuff that Google was producing.

286
00:29:09,040 --> 00:29:12,960
And then you run into other things that some classrooms had an echo in them.

287
00:29:13,440 --> 00:29:15,440
Again, these buildings are of a certain age.

288
00:29:15,600 --> 00:29:18,800
One part of the building that we're in was built in the 15th century.

289
00:29:19,600 --> 00:29:25,040
So you have to think of how can we retain the architecture and yet update the whole stuff.

290
00:29:25,360 --> 00:29:28,160
So there are people around who know this kind of thing.

291
00:29:28,800 --> 00:29:34,400
We had two classrooms which professors refused in principle ever to use because there was an echo.

292
00:29:35,120 --> 00:29:40,400
And we had experts come in and they said, in these two classrooms there's a reverberation of more than three seconds.

293
00:29:40,960 --> 00:29:46,560
Now you think about it, the students come from around the world, they're studying in Italian.

294
00:29:46,960 --> 00:29:49,280
For a lot of the students it might be their fifth language.

295
00:29:50,400 --> 00:29:56,400
The professors come from around the world, the language that they're instructing in is Italian and it might be their third or fourth language.

296
00:29:56,880 --> 00:30:03,040
So the professor is speaking with an accent, the students are listening with an accent, and there's an echo in the room.

297
00:30:04,720 --> 00:30:11,760
So again, we brought in these experts and it's incredibly inexpensive to do this, and even esthetically you can do it well.

298
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:17,920
So we put in sound absorption panels, esthetically on the walls and in the ceiling, and changed the lighting a bit.

299
00:30:18,560 --> 00:30:25,120
And one professor who said he would never teach in that classroom said, Now this is my favorite classroom.

300
00:30:25,520 --> 00:30:27,520
I spent half of my life in classrooms.

301
00:30:28,160 --> 00:30:32,800
I never said I had a favorite classroom, but now that's the way even students were reacting to that.

302
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:34,080
So we studied color.

303
00:30:35,200 --> 00:30:36,560
We studied light intensity.

304
00:30:37,120 --> 00:30:43,040
And so they say 500 lumens per square meter is ideal for study and research.

305
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:50,880
The colors that aid conversation, life, energy are yellow and orange and red.

306
00:30:51,520 --> 00:30:56,000
So we repainted everything without toxic chemicals in the colors.

307
00:30:56,400 --> 00:30:58,400
And again, you could see the difference.

308
00:30:58,720 --> 00:31:03,360
So even business technology will talk about creating a space of joy.

309
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:08,160
So I don't know how many students will get up and say, Oh boy, I'm going to class today.

310
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:12,080
But you do create an environment that is enjoyable to be in.

311
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:14,240
The chairs are comfortable to sit in.

312
00:31:14,560 --> 00:31:15,920
The colors keep you awake.

313
00:31:16,320 --> 00:31:17,920
You can hear everything that's being said.

314
00:31:18,480 --> 00:31:25,680
Another funny thing that we put into the main conference room was this is when Matthew first came was we tech up the main conference room.

315
00:31:26,240 --> 00:31:30,480
So with all the standard technology and all, but we put in five rotating cameras.

316
00:31:31,440 --> 00:31:35,360
And one of the curious things with this, this was just interesting to see on the big screen.

317
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:39,360
And of course, we're connecting with the world, those who wanted to see the thing online.

318
00:31:39,920 --> 00:31:43,600
But the cameras would then rotate around the conference hall.

319
00:31:44,160 --> 00:31:48,560
So students and professors who are sitting and listening would have the camera on them all of a sudden.

320
00:31:48,880 --> 00:31:50,000
So what was one of the effects?

321
00:31:50,320 --> 00:31:53,120
Less sleeping during conferences and all that stuff.

322
00:31:53,520 --> 00:31:54,720
So there was a fun aspect.

323
00:31:55,280 --> 00:31:56,640
There was an academic aspect.

324
00:31:57,840 --> 00:32:00,320
There was an energy aspect to the study and all that.

325
00:32:00,640 --> 00:32:06,240
And then when, as I say, when COVID came along., Google again did as many companies did a generous thing.

326
00:32:07,280 --> 00:32:17,840
They allowed the full extent of Google Education Plus for all Google Education users, which means that you can record the sessions without having to pay extra.

327
00:32:18,240 --> 00:32:26,640
For our students, that was really quite rich because they could listen to the lecture a second time in Italian and pick it up whatever they were missing.

328
00:32:27,120 --> 00:32:32,160
And what we found was they came back to the next lesson full of clear questions.

329
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:39,760
And the professors themselves were shocked that all of a sudden the students are intelligent, but they had the chance to listen to it a second time.

330
00:32:40,160 --> 00:32:46,960
So again, you're going to say, this is technology, pretty basic, pretty simple, but it transformed the quality in the classroom.

331
00:32:47,280 --> 00:32:48,720
And professors commented on that.

332
00:32:49,200 --> 00:32:50,720
The work the students are doing is better.

333
00:32:51,280 --> 00:32:52,880
The papers they're writing are better.

334
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:54,160
The instruction is better.

335
00:32:54,480 --> 00:32:56,000
They're coming back with better questions.

336
00:32:56,640 --> 00:33:01,520
So again, you saw the intelligent use of technology had great human effect.

337
00:33:01,680 --> 00:33:02,560
It's about the human being.

338
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:03,920
It's about the human context.

339
00:33:04,320 --> 00:33:06,640
If it serves that, it can do a great job.

340
00:33:07,120 --> 00:33:14,880
And then it was just step by step from that level of technology, kind of a very human quality technology, to the next step that we're talking about here.

341
00:33:15,280 --> 00:33:16,800
So you just see this multiplier effect.

342
00:33:17,200 --> 00:33:21,200
And again, as I would say, the church is the group that the church is created for good.

343
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:28,320
It should be on the cutting edge of using this stuff for good, showing others the possibilities that's in the technology.

344
00:33:29,280 --> 00:33:30,160
That's so extraordinary.

345
00:33:31,040 --> 00:33:47,280
And Matthew, working in the tech sphere and seeing also how quickly this is evolving and hearing from Father David that these changes are radically transforming in such a positive way the learning environment and the educational format for students from around the world.

346
00:33:47,600 --> 00:33:48,800
Where do you see this technology going?

347
00:33:49,040 --> 00:33:54,960
I mean, we see, so you guys are digitizing books and bringing that and vectorizing it and making that knowledge base available to the world.

348
00:33:55,920 --> 00:33:56,960
What other plans do you have?

349
00:33:57,600 --> 00:33:59,120
Where do you see this going in the coming years?

350
00:33:59,920 --> 00:34:10,560
Well, I mean, I think ultimately, I mean, following David said it, I mean, what we're really trying to figure out is what is the kind of most optimal way, one, to kind of disseminate this information and make sure it's practically useful to as many people as possible.

351
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:12,800
I mean, this is this is kind of what really drives the company.

352
00:34:12,960 --> 00:34:20,880
The quest to build and scale Catholic AI, which is our primary mission, and as we add a secondary mission to digitize the patchwork of the church, because that service, the primary mission.

353
00:34:22,080 --> 00:34:31,360
This is really just about making sure that all of this great insight, right, that very few of us got a chance to go to a seminary or get a master, study, study theology and philosophy, right?

354
00:34:31,680 --> 00:34:37,120
And so we know the enormous benefit that comes from taking that time away to study these, these things.

355
00:34:37,360 --> 00:34:40,480
And for a lot of people never had that opportunity, it's it's very unfortunate.

356
00:34:40,640 --> 00:34:43,600
And some will say this has been a kind of a very clerical lay divide, right?

357
00:34:43,920 --> 00:34:52,560
And so what I see is a real kind of exciting opportunity here is by making all this information available, distilling it for people, is that we can kind of close that gap a little bit, right?

358
00:34:52,800 --> 00:35:01,440
So anyone who has, you know, questions, you know, existential questions or wants to probe church teaching more deeply, well, now they have a place they can go and they can do that, right?

359
00:35:01,760 --> 00:35:03,600
And the AI can patiently work with them, right?

360
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:04,960
And they can move at their own pace.

361
00:35:05,120 --> 00:35:07,600
And this is something so when we talk about the future next decade.

362
00:35:08,080 --> 00:35:15,440
One thing I'm really excited about is the opportunities for AI to actually be able to adapt its pedagogical approach for each student, right?

363
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:17,760
To help them learn more effectively.

364
00:35:18,160 --> 00:35:33,920
And I think, you know, if you think of the old traditional paradigm, I think if we actually go back to the University of Paris, I think when Thomas Quantas was teaching, it was very different, but in the modern paradigms, you may have, you know, in maybe a high school, you got fifty students, you got one teacher, they have a curriculum that they need to teach and they do their thing, right?

365
00:35:34,480 --> 00:35:35,280
They teach that curriculum.

366
00:35:35,360 --> 00:35:37,600
And of course, those fifty students are really, really unique.

367
00:35:37,840 --> 00:35:38,640
They're all different places.

368
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:44,320
So sometimes that particular way of teaching is not overly effective, maybe for some, but not for everyone.

369
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:47,200
And that's just, unfortunately, that's just the best we've been able to do.

370
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:52,720
You know, ideally, I think everyone would prefer that there was a much smaller, you know, teacher to student ratio, right?

371
00:35:52,880 --> 00:35:54,480
So they can get more personalized education.

372
00:35:54,560 --> 00:35:55,280
But how do we do this?

373
00:35:55,360 --> 00:35:56,560
It just seems practically ineffective.

374
00:35:56,640 --> 00:36:00,000
Well, now with something like AI, we can do one to one, right?

375
00:36:00,160 --> 00:36:17,840
And in principle, if you have a curriculum and you provide the AI access to it and you use something like voice mode, you get the student can actually talk to and interact with the AI and the AI can actually teach and based on how the students receive and being able to explain the concepts back to the AI can say, is this working or is it not working?

376
00:36:17,920 --> 00:36:22,320
And if it's not working, it can try, let me try adapting my pedagogical approach until it finds one that works really well.

377
00:36:22,480 --> 00:36:30,800
So there are leaders like Khan Academy, like Sal Khan, who have been demonstrating how this technology can be used to kind of really lift people out of poverty.

378
00:36:31,200 --> 00:36:32,480
Khan Academy is an incredible example.

379
00:36:32,720 --> 00:36:35,040
It's very, very big in India.

380
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:49,360
And I think the Catholic Church, which has been a pioneer in education right from its very beginning, there's a real opportunity for us to kind of establish ourselves as a leader again by because primarily because we don't look at education as a business, right?

381
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:54,000
I mean, this is something we're doing is something to advance the common good.

382
00:36:54,320 --> 00:36:55,280
We care about the human person.

383
00:36:55,520 --> 00:37:08,720
And so we can afford to kind of, kind of move those business incentives aside, the economic incentives aside, even the political and ideological things aside and say, listen, ultimately what we want is for every person to flourish.

384
00:37:09,120 --> 00:37:14,960
So what tools and technologies, what approaches can we use to most effectively ensure that that's the result?

385
00:37:15,040 --> 00:37:23,920
And so because of that open mindedness and that kind of mission first approach to education, I think that there's a real opportunity for the church to be an early adopter in this technology and to demonstrate its effectiveness.

386
00:37:24,640 --> 00:37:33,200
One of the great advantages of what we're talking about in terms of the AI technology is that we're talking about a closed system of research.

387
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:35,520
So what do I mean by that?

388
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:43,760
If you're the Magisterium site or the Vulgate site, you're not getting what Uncle Bob is thinking about Jesus now.

389
00:37:44,640 --> 00:37:50,720
You're getting the classic texts, the teaching of the church, and so it's a reliable source of research.

390
00:37:51,520 --> 00:37:57,440
And the second thing is people would worry about, well, if AI is teaching me, am I going to go off track?

391
00:37:57,600 --> 00:38:00,160
Well, again, this is a bit of the challenge of putting together a thing.

392
00:38:00,320 --> 00:38:08,800
But on the other hand, you say, do all of us believe everything that every teacher has told us from the beginning to the end, that no teacher has ever made a mistake?

393
00:38:08,960 --> 00:38:10,160
Well, you know, welcome to life.

394
00:38:10,560 --> 00:38:16,080
You're going to hear different sides and different opinions, but it's the human being that has to put the pieces together, you know.

395
00:38:16,640 --> 00:38:34,000
So there's a challenge for the these technologies that we're talking about to make them as reliable, intelligent as possible to serve human beings, but not to be too preoccupied about, is there going to be a mistake here or a mistake there?

396
00:38:34,480 --> 00:38:40,240
If I decide to do some weird thing because of something I saw on YouTube, there's something wrong with me.

397
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:42,800
You know, there's not something wrong with the technology, you know.

398
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:49,520
And Father David, how do you see education changing as we, you know, as we continue to progress into the AI era.

399
00:38:49,680 --> 00:38:58,560
There's a lot of students, you know, who are, you know, in high school now, kids in college, and they're, you know, they're working on things, they're trying to, you know, secure their future, work towards getting a job.

400
00:38:58,800 --> 00:39:00,480
And of course, that landscape is changing.

401
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:17,120
How do you see the role of education, the role of universities changing to guide, to, you know, to form students, but also to prepare them for what could be an uncertain and somewhat, you know, daunting future, but also one that has the potential, you know, for more time to study, to form, to, you know, all the upside as well.

402
00:39:17,840 --> 00:39:20,880
I don't know how well universities can do that.

403
00:39:22,160 --> 00:39:25,440
There is a certain aspect where universities are reactionary.

404
00:39:26,240 --> 00:39:30,320
Something is going on, now we'll study it, then maybe we can work on a solution.

405
00:39:30,960 --> 00:39:33,040
But to prepare and advance for it, we don't know.

406
00:39:33,520 --> 00:39:38,080
I don't think universities and schools are best adapted to do that.

407
00:39:38,720 --> 00:39:43,360
Again, and we don't know what the next five years of this technology will bring.

408
00:39:44,080 --> 00:39:51,760
Certainly a preoccupation is the job market, what's going to happen with so many jobs with advances in AI and all this stuff.

409
00:39:52,560 --> 00:39:57,280
And one of the classic principles of Catholic education has always been the development of the human being.

410
00:39:58,480 --> 00:40:00,320
So it's the full development of the human being.

411
00:40:00,720 --> 00:40:09,920
So it's being abreast of what's going my identity attached to technology or am I getting too depressed about something that's going on?

412
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:17,200
The creativity of the human being entering this world with creativity, with energy, looking for new possibilities.

413
00:40:17,440 --> 00:40:21,200
It isn't the first time that we're going through a kind of a social upheaval.

414
00:40:21,760 --> 00:40:31,200
And so it's going to ask for mature people to face this with honesty, to walk through the fears and the preoccupations and the concerns, to maintain a level of justice.

415
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:35,280
And the classic example that the current Pope refers to is Leo XIII.

416
00:40:35,760 --> 00:40:39,040
At the height of industrialization, people were losing jobs everywhere.

417
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:40,240
There were no labor laws.

418
00:40:40,880 --> 00:40:44,800
You could be forced to work 16 hours a day at 12 years of age and all this.

419
00:40:45,200 --> 00:40:54,000
So it took a bit of time to catch up with that, to create the laws, systems, ages, age limits for work and retirement ages and all that.

420
00:40:54,640 --> 00:40:57,040
That took most of the 20th century to refine.

421
00:40:57,680 --> 00:41:00,960
Pension laws came in in the mid 20th century.

422
00:41:02,080 --> 00:41:06,000
Healthcare, universal healthcare came in after the Second World War in many countries.

423
00:41:06,640 --> 00:41:08,640
So this stuff you're reacting to.

424
00:41:09,120 --> 00:41:16,320
So as best we can prepare the human being for facing the current day without fears, without.

425
00:41:16,320 --> 00:41:23,440
about overconcerns or preoccupations of what might not be, but to enter it with courage and imagination.

426
00:41:23,920 --> 00:41:26,240
I think that's the best that we can hope for from education.

427
00:41:27,280 --> 00:41:33,760
And I think, you know, this might be an opportunity for us to kind of get back to first principles when it comes to universities, right?

428
00:41:34,480 --> 00:41:40,960
I think too much, even Catholic universities have kind of given way to this pressure, right?

429
00:41:43,040 --> 00:41:47,120
That, you know, the purpose of a university today is for you to achieve a degree, right?

430
00:41:47,360 --> 00:41:51,200
To get a piece of paper so that you can qualify for a job, right?

431
00:41:51,680 --> 00:41:55,360
And then so, you know, you need a job because you need to get a mortgage, right?

432
00:41:55,680 --> 00:41:58,720
And then you need to be able to have money in order to put your kids through college, right?

433
00:41:58,880 --> 00:42:00,560
And so it's this kind of cycle.

434
00:42:01,600 --> 00:42:04,560
And but that's not what education is supposed to be about, right?

435
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:09,760
And I think for if you look at like when I was going through high school, I think probably a lot of people can share this experience.

436
00:42:09,920 --> 00:42:14,320
I mean, I didn't spend a lot of time exploring what my, my true gifts are.

437
00:42:15,280 --> 00:42:16,880
I just didn't have much of an imagination for it.

438
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:24,560
I mean, I knew there were some things I liked more than others and stuff, but I, I didn't spend a lot of time and certainly I didn't feel in the kind of the classroom environment that we spend a lot of time focusing on math.

439
00:42:24,880 --> 00:42:26,800
Let's figure out who you are and what you're good at, right?

440
00:42:26,960 --> 00:42:30,000
And so that we can kind of figure out how you can hone those gifts and skills to serve.

441
00:42:30,160 --> 00:42:30,960
And that makes sense, right?

442
00:42:31,200 --> 00:42:32,720
Especially when you have one teacher for fifty students.

443
00:42:33,760 --> 00:42:35,120
You have to be reasonable in your expectations.

444
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:42,480
So, you know, I kind of feel like that universities can get back to what they're supposed to be doing, which is basically focus on helping people develop.

445
00:42:43,920 --> 00:42:46,880
And so in some ways, I think this will require some pedagogical changes.

446
00:42:47,040 --> 00:42:50,000
I mean, in one, in one regard, I've already spoken to this.

447
00:42:50,160 --> 00:42:58,800
I think a lot of the actual teaching will probably happen between the AI and the individual, mainly because the AI's probably more and more effective at it.

448
00:42:59,520 --> 00:43:02,720
But that doesn't mean that the classroom is no longer useful.

449
00:43:03,200 --> 00:43:03,920
I think absolutely.

450
00:43:04,080 --> 00:43:04,880
It still will be.

451
00:43:05,040 --> 00:43:07,280
It's just that the nature of the classroom, I think, will probably change, right?

452
00:43:07,840 --> 00:43:18,880
And so instead of that being a place where information is basically kind of downloaded to me and we all sit there quietly and make notes in our books, it'll be a place where I can then having now digested all of these insights, right?

453
00:43:19,040 --> 00:43:27,600
And now having effectively understood them more better because I've worked with AI to make sure and it's validated that I have understood these concepts, I can now go into a classroom environment and share them with others.

454
00:43:27,760 --> 00:43:34,640
And we can actually look at problems together and I can hear other people's opinions and insights and I can actually learn what that, how that process should be done, right?

455
00:43:35,200 --> 00:43:39,200
How do I respectfully ensure my ideas are made known and how do I respectfully listen to other people's ideas?

456
00:43:39,840 --> 00:43:42,720
How do we effectively work together constructively on problems?

457
00:43:43,120 --> 00:43:43,200
You know?

458
00:43:43,360 --> 00:43:56,000
And so I think if the classroom starts to resemble that more, I ultimately think that that will help a lot with the socialization skills, which I think are becoming increasingly more difficult to instill, especially as people are, most of young people's lives are media through devices.

459
00:43:56,960 --> 00:44:00,000
And I actually think practically that will actually help prepare them for the job market.

460
00:44:00,320 --> 00:44:07,040
I mean, because, you know, one thing is, whether someone has a degree or not, is matters to me less and less as time goes.

461
00:44:07,680 --> 00:44:10,320
What I care more about is, is the person a good and honest person?

462
00:44:10,880 --> 00:44:11,760
Are they a hard worker?

463
00:44:12,320 --> 00:44:16,320
And when they're working as part of our team, how effectively do they work with others?

464
00:44:16,640 --> 00:44:22,080
And so I think if the classroom environment focuses on hinging more of those skills, that actually will effectively prepare them better for the job market.

465
00:44:24,640 --> 00:44:25,120
That's that.

466
00:44:25,600 --> 00:44:36,000
That's very exciting and also kind of wild to think about the students, Gene, and being able to really focus on those core values and those core skills.

467
00:44:37,040 --> 00:44:44,080
Matthew, as we prepare to launch the Alexandria digitization hub and we, you know, two scanners coming in, we have a bunch more coming in.

468
00:44:44,880 --> 00:44:48,880
It seems as we're scanning more, it does seem still that we're at the tip of the iceberg.

469
00:44:49,360 --> 00:44:52,560
How much more work do we have to do to digitize the patrimony of the church?

470
00:44:52,640 --> 00:44:56,000
I mean, how many books are we talking about, hopefully, in the next few years?

471
00:44:57,120 --> 00:44:57,920
How does that look like?

472
00:44:58,080 --> 00:45:02,240
So for our audience who, you know, is wants to keep up to date with that progress.

473
00:45:03,680 --> 00:45:07,120
Well, I mean, you know, I think it's it's I mean, hundreds of thousands, millions of books.

474
00:45:07,280 --> 00:45:10,080
I mean, so the patrimony of the church is not small.

475
00:45:10,640 --> 00:45:16,160
So in that respect it's daunting and it'll take a lot of time, but I think it's worth it.

476
00:45:16,400 --> 00:45:16,880
It's worth it.

477
00:45:17,040 --> 00:45:20,800
I mean, so and again, we have to, we have to like, it's important to, you know, to approach this.

478
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:24,000
I mean, there's there's a kind of critical doctrine and dogma, right?

479
00:45:24,400 --> 00:45:29,280
There's the works of the fathers of the church, you know, and so that's been our primary focus.

480
00:45:30,480 --> 00:45:32,480
And we're getting deeper and deeper into that.

481
00:45:32,960 --> 00:45:41,440
I feel like by the end of the summer, we'll probably have the collecctive works of all the fathers and doctors of the church made available to anyone in the world in their own language for free, which is pretty exciting.

482
00:45:41,840 --> 00:45:44,480
We already have a kind of core magisterial teaching is in there.

483
00:45:44,720 --> 00:45:53,680
Now we're going back to the more ancient popes and trying to digitize their kind of ancient writings and their ancient encyclicals and things to ensure that their voices are added to the modern magisterium.

484
00:45:54,160 --> 00:45:57,280
So I think in the next year, we'll get a lot done.

485
00:45:58,160 --> 00:46:07,680
But one thing that kind of excites me about this project and you get this experience, like when you walk into one of these libraries, is you know that a bunch of those books have not been opened in a hundred years, two hundred years.

486
00:46:07,760 --> 00:46:11,200
And if you were to ask a librarian what's in that book, I mean, they could tell you what the title is, right?

487
00:46:11,520 --> 00:46:12,560
But they don't even know, right?

488
00:46:13,040 --> 00:46:21,840
So that for me is exciting because I think there's probably a lot of brilliant, brilliant minds, brilliant voices from the past who have a lot to say even to our contemporary world.

489
00:46:22,640 --> 00:46:29,680
But because nobody knows what they what they have to say because those books have not been opened, unfortunately their insights are just kind of lost to us.

490
00:46:29,760 --> 00:46:37,600
And so what I what I'm excited about is this process of digitizing these these books and then of course making them available through tools like Madison Reading Volga.

491
00:46:37,920 --> 00:46:42,800
What's kind of really exciting about me is that someone may be asking a pretty typical theological question.

492
00:46:43,360 --> 00:46:48,400
And because we've now digitized these books, these people's insights, those insights can now be brought to bear.

493
00:46:48,480 --> 00:46:49,920
They may start popping up in any newspaper.

494
00:46:50,560 --> 00:47:01,440
And so what does it, it almost increases the surface area for research, which is exciting, I think, for in academia, but it also ensures that those insights may be particularly useful to to to to to someone, right?

495
00:47:01,600 --> 00:47:13,600
And so that could help them hopefully constructively in their life and it it may lead to devotions to to to to to to to to to to some of these scholars and and we may have to revalue the work and say actually that their contributions are much more significant than we thought and and maybe we should spend more time thinking about it, right?

496
00:47:13,760 --> 00:47:16,480
And so there could be more spiritual devotions and more research that happens as a result.

497
00:47:16,960 --> 00:47:37,760
And and Father David, how do you see bringing bringing to the modern era all these writers that have you know have kind of been falling asleep for the last couple of centuries if you know if these books have haven't been opened or if scholarships focus on a specific niche topic at this moment, how do you think it's going to inform the dialogue within the church and the dialogue with the wider society having all these voices coming up to air now?

498
00:47:38,560 --> 00:47:52,880
Well, I think it can only help because there's to make an analogous comment, a country that has only two or three political parties only represents the people that have those kinds of opinions.

499
00:47:53,520 --> 00:48:00,400
But if you're in a country with 100 million people, you probably need more opinions than two or three to cover what the people are about.

500
00:48:00,880 --> 00:48:01,600
So the same thing here.

501
00:48:05,520 --> 00:48:08,400
a democratic factor in this.

502
00:48:08,720 --> 00:48:19,360
The more people that have more access to more resources, letting people develop along the spirit of their own genius, their own interests and all, it enhances social conversation.

503
00:48:20,400 --> 00:48:33,600
It enhances the possibilities for broader justice, broader human understanding, whether it's ecumenism among Christian religions or other religions or even just other ideological groups, other nations, other countries.

504
00:48:34,240 --> 00:48:37,920
It just enhances those possibilities because it doesn't limit the people.

505
00:48:38,240 --> 00:48:49,600
This is always a criticism of a classroom is that classroom has bright students, not so bright students and a bunch in the middle, people interested in physical labor, people interested in that.

506
00:48:49,920 --> 00:48:56,080
Well, the classroom at one teacher and fifty students can only represent kind of one basic position.

507
00:48:56,800 --> 00:49:03,680
This kind of structure allows for many kind of geniuses, minds, interests to be expressed and developed.

508
00:49:04,000 --> 00:49:06,160
So it enhances social conversation.

509
00:49:07,360 --> 00:49:08,080
That's fantastic.

510
00:49:09,440 --> 00:49:22,160
And I guess my last question is how how do we think, you know, for the for the wider church as this as this technology, you know, continues to bring more and more of these documents to light and shares them around the world.

511
00:49:22,880 --> 00:49:26,080
What are your hopes for the church adopting this more, you know, more widely, more broadly?

512
00:49:26,240 --> 00:49:28,400
There's many, many pontifical universities around the world.

513
00:49:28,720 --> 00:49:31,760
There's, you know, universities around the world who might not have access to this yet.

514
00:49:32,400 --> 00:49:34,800
What are your hopes and dreams as we look towards the future?

515
00:49:35,440 --> 00:49:47,520
Well, I mean, the last number of years has given indication that it's foolish to make long term plans, you know, beyond three months, we just don't know what's going to happen out there.

516
00:49:48,000 --> 00:49:50,960
So in one sense, it's running with this.

517
00:49:51,200 --> 00:49:55,360
You know, there's a title of a book, I think, in the 1960s, Are you running with me, Jesus?

518
00:49:56,400 --> 00:50:04,960
And it's with devotion, with faith, with all goodwill to run forward with this stuff, to use this stuff as best we can.

519
00:50:05,600 --> 00:50:12,560
But for me to predict where it's going, we wouldn't have predicted three years ago that we would be where we are now with the digitizing project.

520
00:50:13,280 --> 00:50:17,040
So to imagine in the future, I'm not sure if that's the best question to ask.

521
00:50:17,360 --> 00:50:22,880
It's a logical question to ask, but how do we live fully in the moment that we're in?

522
00:50:23,600 --> 00:50:24,880
It's kind of that for me.

523
00:50:25,680 --> 00:50:26,400
I have to agree.

524
00:50:27,280 --> 00:50:41,280
I think I hope that with this new pontificate, this is becoming more central focus is constituently what needs to be in place in order to ensure that people flourish.

525
00:50:42,640 --> 00:50:49,920
And then we kind of work backwards and say, how can this new technology now and possibly in the future help contribute to that flourishing?

526
00:50:51,120 --> 00:51:10,800
And I think if we think of it more like that, given the power of this technology and the import now that it's going beyond these AI systems, disembodied AI systems which live in large computing clusters to now being embodied in robots, meaning they're actually able to physically interact in our world, we start thinking about the import of that it's going to touch every facet of society, right?

527
00:51:11,040 --> 00:51:23,760
So if we have a clear understanding of where we, where human beings should be headed, how this technology can help enhance flourishing, then we can make sure that we're basically focusing our society's resources and developing the technology in a way that will optimize flourishing, right?

528
00:51:24,000 --> 00:51:26,320
As opposed to impeding it, right?

529
00:51:26,480 --> 00:51:34,560
By, you know, like, and one thing that I get concerned about is, you know, as AI robots get deployed more and more, it's likely to be the case that people have more time on their hands.

530
00:51:34,880 --> 00:51:39,680
And I think that's a good thing, but for many people, it could, it could actually not be a good thing.

531
00:51:39,840 --> 00:51:44,320
It could end up impeding the flourishing because they may choose to use that time to do nothing but play, right?

532
00:51:44,560 --> 00:51:50,000
Or spend time in VR worlds or spend more time in their devices or God willing, God forbid, looking at porn or something like that.

533
00:51:50,080 --> 00:52:04,720
I mean, so I think, you know, given that's likely to be the outcome for a lot of people, I think it's really, it's critical for us in the church to remember, you know, that it's a, you know, flourishing, but also the importance of evangelization, right?

534
00:52:04,960 --> 00:52:06,640
We have to make sure that we're at the gospel.

535
00:52:06,800 --> 00:52:09,440
We're finding new ways to deliver the gospel in ways that are effective.

536
00:52:10,080 --> 00:52:19,680
We have to find ways to reach those people who maybe who don't know about the gospel and and the benefits of a Christian way of life, how and how enriching it is because maybe they've never seen it or it's been explained to them.

537
00:52:19,920 --> 00:52:27,920
If we do that more effectively, then people won't just like by default end up disappearing into these virtual gaming worlds simply because they don't know what they're supposed to be doing with their lives, right?

538
00:52:28,080 --> 00:52:33,360
Because they haven't had a chance to discern what their gifts are and figure out how to put those gifts at the service of others and of God.

539
00:52:33,920 --> 00:52:46,400
And so this is why I think what, like, it's been a real, like, honor and privilege to work with Father David because, um, you know, being someone who has worked in technology now, you know, for a good period of time, I get very hopeful about the future of it.

540
00:52:46,640 --> 00:52:55,120
But one of the things I get worried about is just how do I effectively, um, deploy the technology and, and, and how do I, how do I even make a case for it?

541
00:52:55,280 --> 00:53:03,760
And so when you meet people like Father David who are who understand the problems and are willing to try new things out, right?

542
00:53:04,560 --> 00:53:10,800
It's exciting because because then you get a chance to show people, to demonstrate that it can work.

543
00:53:11,040 --> 00:53:25,200
And ironically, maybe this is not entirely fair to say, but I have found in Rome in particular, seeing is believing, you know, and at least when it comes to like technology and things.

544
00:53:25,440 --> 00:53:32,240
And I think so the more that we can demonstrate the benefits of the technology, people can come, they can see it for themselves, they can interact with it, they can touch with it.

545
00:53:32,240 --> 00:53:35,600
I think the more likely it will, it's likely to scale and be adopted elsewhere.

546
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:44,720
And that's one of the reasons why I'm so proud of what Father David has done at the Pontifical Orientals to do, because it stands in Rome as probably one of the most advanced universities, probably in Italy.

547
